% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Chapter 5, section 5.8 ("Decompiled layered rules-and-exceptions",
%         with the Bob/Mary negation-as-failure trace).
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% Layered rules-and-exceptions with negation as failure. The general rule
% punishes thieves, defeated by the exception for minors, which is itself
% defeated by the exception-to-the-exception for violent offenders. Each layer
% is a separate clause whose body negates the next layer down, so a violent
% minor thief is punished again (the double exception cancels out). The negated
% sub-sentence is placed on its own indented line after "it is not the case
% that" so the parser binds the shared "the person" variable correctly.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*a person* is a thief.
*a person* is a minor.
*a person* is violent.
*a person* should be punished.
*a person* is an exception to the punishment rule.
*a person* is an exception to the exception to the punishment rule.

the knowledge base thieves punishment includes:

a person should be punished
    if the person is a thief
    and it is not the case that
        the person is an exception to the punishment rule.

a person is an exception to the punishment rule
    if the person is a minor
    and it is not the case that
        the person is an exception to the exception to the punishment rule.

a person is an exception to the exception to the punishment rule
    if the person is violent.

scenario bob_mary is:
    bob is a thief.
    mary is a thief.
    mary is a minor.
    tom is a thief.
    tom is a minor.
    tom is violent.
    punishment expects answers ["bob should be punished", "tom should be punished"].

query punishment is:
    which person should be punished.
